      "id": "1357",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1357,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The non-past (or dictionary) form expresses habitual or future actions, while the past form expresses completed actions or past states. For る-Verbs, change the final る to た to form the past tense. Both the non-past and past forms are casual, non-polite forms used in conversation or other grammar patterns.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 950,
          "slug": "verb-non-past",
          "title": "Verbs (Non-past)",
          "meaning": "(Non-past)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 895,
          "slug": "る-verb-past",
          "title": "る-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "る - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1335",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1335,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The dictionary form of a る-verb is its base form, expressing current, habitual, or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The past form (-た form) describes completed actions or past states (e.g., 食べた = 'I ate'). Both forms are commonly used in casual speech and various grammar patterns requiring plain verb forms, such as ～と思います ('I think').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 12,
          "slug": "る-Verbs",
          "title": "る-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "る + ます (Ichidan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 895,
          "slug": "る-verb-past",
          "title": "る-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "る - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1362",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1362,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The る-Verb (Past) expresses completed actions or past states. Unlike English, verbs can modify nouns directly, similar to い-Adjectives. For example, 食べたケーキ means 'the cake [I] ate.' This applies mainly to the plain/casual form, while the polite ます form is used at the end of clauses or sentences.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 357,
          "slug": "verb-た-noun",
          "title": "Verb［た・ている］+ Noun",
          "meaning": "Verb modified noun, Relative clause",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 895,
          "slug": "る-verb-past",
          "title": "る-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "る - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
