        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1342",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1342,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The past form of verbs expresses a completed action or an action that took place in the past, similar to the English '-ed.' It is a plain form often used in casual conversation or as part of other grammatical structures. The past form differs for る-Verbs and う-Verbs (e.g., 食べる becomes 食べた, and 買う becomes 買った), but the meaning of the conjugation is the same.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 895,
          "slug": "る-verb-past",
          "title": "る-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "る - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 41,
          "slug": "う-verb-past",
          "title": "う-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "う - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1346",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1346,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "These forms are used with る-Verbs to express the idea of having done or not having done a particular action in the past. The difference is whether the meaning is affirmative ('did') or negative ('did not'). For example, 食べた means 'I ate,' while 食べなかった means 'I did not eat.' Both are plain forms used in casual speech or as part of other grammatical forms.",
        "first_relatable": {
